      en: "Dong Amphan National Park is a massive protected area spanning approximately 2,000 square kilometers in Attapeu Province, in the extreme southeast of Laos along the Vietnamese border. The terrain is characterized by rugged mountains, steep valleys, and dense, largely untouched primary tropical forests. Recognized as one of the most intact forest ecosystems remaining in Southeast Asia, the park is a crucial sanctuary for endangered wildlife, including Asian elephants, clouded leopards, tigers, and gibbons. Its most famous geographical feature is the stunning volcanic crater lake, Nong Fa Lake. The severe isolation and lack of infrastructure have naturally shielded Dong Amphan's incredible biodiversity from large-scale human interference."
